// 7. Accessing array in nested loops with incorrect bounds
public void nestedLoopAccess() {
    int[] data = {1, 2, 3, 4};
    for (int i = 0; i <= data.length; i++) { // Loop runs one over
        for (int j = 0; j < data.length; j++) {
            data[i] = data[j]; // i can be out of bounds
        }
    }
}

Vulnerability Report

1) Vulnerability Description
The code contains an index out-of-bounds access in a nested loop, which results in reading or writing beyond the valid bounds of the array. This can lead to runtime exceptions or undefined behavior, potentially compromising program stability and security.

2) Vulnerable Code
```java
public void nestedLoopAccess() {
    int[] data = {1, 2, 3, 4};
    for (int i = 0; i <= data.length; i++) { // Loop runs one iteration too many
        for (int j = 0; j < data.length; j++) {
            data[i] = data[j]; // i can be out of bounds when i == data.length
        }
    }
}
```

3) Root Cause
The outer loop uses `i <= data.length`, causing `i` to reach `data.length` on the last iteration. Since array indices are 0-based, valid indices are from 0 to `data.length - 1`. Accessing `data[i]` when `i == data.length` results in an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.

4) Impact
- Runtime exceptions leading to program crashes.
- Potential undefined behavior if array access is manipulated maliciously.
- Possible denial-of-service if unhandled exceptions occur repeatedly.
- Security risks if attackers exploit out-of-bounds accesses (less likely in this specific case but possible in other contexts).

5) Mitigation
- Change the loop condition to `i < data.length` to ensure `i` remains within valid bounds:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
    // ...
}
```
- Add proper bounds checking before array access.
- Use higher-level abstractions or utility methods that handle boundary conditions.
- Implement rigorous testing to identify boundary violations during development.
- Incorporate static analysis tools to detect such issues automatically.

