// 10. Reading from array using incorrect boundary check
public void readArray() {
    int[] array = {10, 20, 30};
    int i = 0;
    while (i <= array.length) { // Includes out of bounds index
        System.out.println(array[i]);
        i++;
    }
}

Vulnerability Report

1) Vulnerability Description:
The code demonstrates an incorrect boundary check when accessing array elements, leading to a potential out-of-bounds access. This situation qualifies as CWE-788: Access of Memory Location After End of Buffer, which can result in runtime exceptions or undefined behavior.

2) Vulnerable Code:
```java
public void readArray() {
    int[] array = {10, 20, 30};
    int i = 0;
    while (i <= array.length) { // Incorrect boundary check
        System.out.println(array[i]);
        i++;
    }
}
```

3) Root Cause:
The loop condition `i <= array.length` causes the index `i` to reach `array.length`, which is beyond the highest valid index (array.length - 1). Since array indices are zero-based, attempting to access `array[array.length]` results in an `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.

4) Impact:
Accessing memory outside the valid array bounds can cause:
- Runtime crashes due to `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.
- Potential security vulnerabilities if exploited to read or modify unintended memory areas in unsafe contexts (although Java's managed environment mitigates direct memory corruption).

5) Mitigation:
- Correct the loop condition to `i < array.length` to ensure indices stay within valid bounds:
```java
while (i < array.length) {
    System.out.println(array[i]);
    i++;
}
```
- Implement proper boundary checks and consider using enhanced for-loops for cleaner and safer iteration:
```java
for (int item : array) {
    System.out.println(item);
}
```
- Always validate indices before array access during iteration to prevent out-of-bounds errors.

