// 9. Double free in multithreaded scenario (not safe)
public class MultithreadedResourceHandler {
    private java.io.InputStream stream;

    public MultithreadedResourceHandler() {
        stream = new java.io.ByteArrayInputStream(new byte[]{});
    }

    public void closeInThread() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                if (stream != null) {
                    stream.close(); // first free
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
            }
        });
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                if (stream != null) {
                    stream.close(); // second free
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
            }
        });
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t1.join();
        t2.join();
    }
}

VULNERABILITY REPORT

1) Vulnerability Description
The code demonstrates a double free vulnerability that occurs when multiple threads concurrently close the same resource without proper synchronization. This can lead to unpredictable behavior, including program crashes or resource corruption.

2) Vulnerable Code
```java
public class MultithreadedResourceHandler {
    private java.io.InputStream stream;

    public MultithreadedResourceHandler() {
        stream = new java.io.ByteArrayInputStream(new byte[]{});
    }

    public void closeInThread() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                if (stream != null) {
                    stream.close(); // first free
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
            }
        });
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                if (stream != null) {
                    stream.close(); // second free
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
            }
        });
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t1.join();
        t2.join();
    }
}
```

3) Root Cause
The core issue stems from multiple threads attempting to close the same `InputStream` instance simultaneously without synchronization. Once the first thread closes the stream, the second thread’s attempt to close it results in a second free of the same resource, leading to a double free scenario. The lack of thread safety checks around the `close()` operation permits this race condition.

4) Impact
- Program instability: Double closing a resource can cause unpredictable behavior, crashes, or data corruption.
- Security implications: Exploiting such race conditions could potentially lead to Denial of Service (DoS) or other vulnerabilities in more complex systems.
- Resource leaks or mismanagement: Although not directly highlighted here, improper handling might complicate resource lifecycle management.

5) Mitigation
- Synchronize access: Use synchronization mechanisms (e.g., synchronized blocks, ReentrantLock) to ensure only one thread can close the resource at a time.
- Check resource state: Implement state checks (e.g., a boolean flag) before closing to prevent multiple closures.
- Use thread-safe classes or patterns: Employ thread-safe wrappers or design patterns that prevent concurrent close operations.
- Avoid shared mutable state: Minimize shared resource access between threads or manage resources within a controlled thread context.

Example mitigation snippet:
```java
public class SafeResourceHandler {
    private final java.io.InputStream stream;
    private final Object lock = new Object();
    private boolean isClosed = false;

    public SafeResourceHandler() {
        stream = new java.io.ByteArrayInputStream(new byte[]{});
    }

    public void closeInThread() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> closeResource());
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> closeResource());
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t1.join();
        t2.join();
    }

    private void closeResource() {
        synchronized (lock) {
            if (!isClosed) {
                try {
                    stream.close();
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    // handle exception
                } finally {
                    isClosed = true;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

