// 4. Concatenating strings without size checks, overwriting buffer
public class BufferOverflowExample4 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] buffer = new String[3];
        buffer[0] = "Hello";
        buffer[1] = "World";

        String longInput = "ThisIsAnExceedinglyLongString";

        // Assigning without checking buffer length
        buffer[2] = longInput; // buffer has only 3 elements, safe but logic error
        for (String s : buffer) {
            System.out.println(s);
        }
    }
}

**Vulnerability Report**

---

### 1) Vulnerability Description
The code demonstrates a potential flaw related to improper handling of array bounds, which can lead to buffer overflows or data corruption. Although Java typically enforces array bounds at runtime, mismanagement of array indices or assumptions about buffer sizes can introduce logical errors, which in lower-level languages could result in memory corruption or security vulnerabilities.

