// 5. Looping through array with user-controlled counter without bounds check
public class LoopRead {
    public static void processArray(int userCount) {
        int[] data = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        for (int i = 0; i <= userCount; i++) { // Possible out-of-bounds
            System.out.println(data[i]);
        }
    }
}

Vulnerability Report

1) Vulnerability Description:
The code performs array access based on user input without proper bounds checking. This can lead to an out-of-bounds read (CWE-125), causing runtime exceptions or undefined behavior, potentially exposing sensitive memory or crashing the application.

2) Vulnerable Code:
```java
public class LoopRead {
    public static void processArray(int userCount) {
        int[] data = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        for (int i = 0; i <= userCount; i++) { // No bounds check against array length
            System.out.println(data[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

3) Root Cause:
The loop iteration variable `i` is controlled by `userCount` without validation. The condition `i <= userCount` allows `i` to exceed `data.length - 1`, leading to attempts to read beyond the array bounds when `userCount` exceeds `data.length - 1`.

4) Impact:
An out-of-bounds read can cause:
- Application crashes with `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.
- Potential exposure to unintended memory or data if the exception is mishandled.
- Security vulnerabilities if exploited to reveal sensitive information or cause denial of service.

5) Mitigation:
- Include a bounds check in the loop:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < Math.min(userCount, data.length); i++) {
    System.out.println(data[i]);
}
```
- Or validate `userCount` before the loop:
```java
if (userCount >= 0 && userCount < data.length) {
    for (int i = 0; i <= userCount; i++) {
        System.out.println(data[i]);
    }
}
```
- Always ensure user-supplied values do not lead to out-of-bounds array access.

