// 2. User role assignment without default case
public class UserManagement {
    public String getUserPermissions(String role) {
        switch (role) {
            case "Admin":
                return "FullAccess";
            case "Editor":
                return "EditContent";
            case "Viewer":
                return "ReadOnly";
            // Missing default; unknown roles default to null
        }
        return null;
    }
}

Vulnerability Report

1) Vulnerability Description:
The code lacks a default case in the switch statement handling user roles. This omission can lead to unexpected behavior when an unknown role is provided, resulting in the method returning null instead of a defined permission value. This pattern qualifies as CWE-478: Missing Default Case in Multiple Condition Expression.

2) Vulnerable Code:
```java
public class UserManagement {
    public String getUserPermissions(String role) {
        switch (role) {
            case "Admin":
                return "FullAccess";
            case "Editor":
                return "EditContent";
            case "Viewer":
                return "ReadOnly";
            // Missing default; unknown roles default to null
        }
        return null;
    }
}
```

3) Root Cause:
The switch statement does not include a default case to handle unrecognized role inputs. As a result, when an input role does not match any of the specified cases, the method proceeds beyond the switch without returning a valid permission, leading to a null value.

4) Impact:
- Returns null for unknown or invalid role inputs, which can cause NullPointerExceptions if the callers do not handle null values properly.
- Leads to unpredictable behavior and potential security issues if permissions are assumed to always be correctly assigned.
- Hinders maintainability and completeness of role handling logic.

5) Mitigation:
- Add a default case to handle any unspecified roles, for example:
  ```java
  default:
      return "NoAccess"; // or throw an exception such as IllegalArgumentException
  ```
- Alternatively, validate role input before the switch statement or handle null inputs explicitly.
- Ensure all possible role inputs are accounted for to prevent unexpected null returns and improve robustness.

