// 2. Web server handling multiple requests, each allocating objects without total memory check
public class RequestHandler {
    private static final int MAX_MEMORY_PER_SESSION = 50_000_000; // bytes
    private int currentMemoryUsage = 0;

    public void handleRequest(List<Integer> dataSizes) {
        for (int size : dataSizes) {
            if (currentMemoryUsage + size > MAX_MEMORY_PER_SESSION) {
                throw new OutOfMemoryError("Memory limit exceeded for session");
            }
            int[] data = new int[size];
            currentMemoryUsage += size;
            // Process data...
        }
    }
}

Vulnerability Report

1. Vulnerability Description
The code dynamically allocates memory for request processing without properly controlling overall memory usage. This can lead to excessive memory consumption, potentially causing application instability or crashes due to unrestrained sequential allocations, making the system susceptible to a CWE-1325: Improperly Controlled Sequential Memory Allocation vulnerability.

2. Vulnerable Code
```java
public class RequestHandler {
    private static final int MAX_MEMORY_PER_SESSION = 50_000_000; // bytes
    private int currentMemoryUsage = 0;

    public void handleRequest(List<Integer> dataSizes) {
