// 5. Passing a mutable custom object without cloning
class UserProfile {
    public String name;
    public int age;

    public UserProfile(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}

public class VulnerableExample5 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UserProfile profile = new UserProfile("John", 30);
        updateProfile(profile);
        System.out.println("Profile after update: " + profile.name + ", " + profile.age);
    }

    public static void updateProfile(UserProfile user) {
        // External method modifies the object
        user.age = 35;
    }
}

Vulnerability Report

1) Vulnerability Description:
The code passes a mutable object (`UserProfile`) directly to an external method (`updateProfile`) without any validation or defensive copying. This exposes the internal state of the object to modification by untrusted or external code, potentially leading to unintended side effects, data integrity issues, or security vulnerabilities.

2) Vulnerable Code:
```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    UserProfile profile = new UserProfile("John", 30);
    updateProfile(profile);
    System.out.println("Profile after update: " + profile.name + ", " + profile.age);
}

public static void updateProfile(UserProfile user) {
    // External method modifies the object
    user.age = 35;
}
```

3) Root Cause:
The root cause is passing a mutable object reference (`UserProfile`) directly to a method that can modify its internal state without any validation or cloning. Since the object is mutable and externally accessible, its state can be altered unexpectedly or maliciously, leading to potential security or integrity issues.

4) Impact:
- Unauthorized modification of user data.
- Loss of data integrity and consistency.
- Increased risk of bugs or malicious exploits if trusted code modifies the object unexpectedly.
- Difficulties in maintaining invariants if external code alters internal state.

5) Mitigation:
- Avoid passing mutable objects directly if the data should remain immutable or controlled.
- Implement defensive copying: clone the object before passing or returning it.
- Use immutable classes for data that should not be changed after creation.
- Validate or restrict modifications in external methods, or enforce access control.
- Example: provide only read-only interfaces or copies to external code to prevent unintended modifications.

