// 8. Shared resource file write without synchronization
import java.io.FileWriter;
import java.io.IOException;

public class FileWriterExample {
    private FileWriter writer;

    public FileWriterExample(String filename) throws IOException {
        this.writer = new FileWriter(filename);
    }

    public void writeLine(String line) throws IOException {
        writer.write(line + "\n"); // Race condition if multiple threads call this
    }

    public void close() throws IOException {
        writer.close();
    }
}

Vulnerability Report

1) Vulnerability Description:
The provided code introduces a race condition when multiple threads invoke the `writeLine()` method simultaneously. This can lead to unpredictable behavior, such as data corruption or lost writes, due to concurrent access to the shared `FileWriter` resource without proper synchronization.

2) Vulnerable Code:
```java
// 8. Shared resource file write without synchronization
import java.io.FileWriter;
import java.io.IOException;

public class FileWriterExample {
    private FileWriter writer;

    public FileWriterExample(String filename) throws IOException {
        this.writer = new FileWriter(filename);
    }

    public void writeLine(String line) throws IOException {
        writer.write(line + "\n"); // Race condition if multiple threads call this
    }

    public void close() throws IOException {
        writer.close();
    }
}
```

3) Root Cause:
The root cause of the vulnerability is the absence of synchronization when multiple threads access the shared `FileWriter` object. Without mechanisms (such as synchronized blocks or locks), concurrent `write()` calls may interleave unpredictably, causing inconsistent or corrupted data in the file.

4) Impact:
- Data corruption: Multiple threads writing simultaneously can result in garbled or incomplete lines.
- Loss of data: Some writes may overwrite or be lost due to overlapping writes.
- Unpredictable behavior: File contents become unreliable, which can compromise data integrity and application correctness.

5) Mitigation:
- Synchronize access to the `writeLine()` method using `synchronized` blocks:

```java
public void writeLine(String line) throws IOException {
    synchronized(this) {
        writer.write(line + "\n");
    }
}
```

- Alternatively, use thread-safe I/O classes or external locking mechanisms.
- Ensure the `close()` method is also synchronized or called after all write operations are complete to prevent closing the stream while writes are ongoing.
- Consider using higher-level concurrency utilities, such as `java.util.concurrent` classes, to manage thread-safe file writing more robustly.

