    private static ConfigManager instance;

    private Properties properties;

    private ConfigManager() {
        this.properties = new Properties();
    }

    public static ConfigManager getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (ConfigManager.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new ConfigManager();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

3. Root Cause
The core issue is the absence of the `volatile` keyword on the `instance` variable. Without `volatile`, the Java Memory Model allows the writes to `instance` to be reordered or cached in a way that other threads may observe an object that is not fully initialized. Consequently, multiple threads can pass the `null` check and create multiple instances, violating the singleton property, or observe a partially constructed object.

